  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Da vs Bal Kishan & Ors. on 26 July, 2018 In The Court Of Samar Vishal, Additional Chief Metropolitan Magistrate - Ii Patiala House Courts, New Delhi Cc No. 39625/2016 Date of Institution : 02.04.2003 Date of reserving judgement : 26.07.2018 Date of pronouncement : 26.07.2018 In re: Delhi Administration / Food Inspector Department of Pfa, Govt. of Nct of Delhi A-20, Lawrence Road Industrial Area, Delhi-110035 ... Complainant versus A-1) Sh. Bal Kishan S/o Sh Laxmi Chand M/s Purna Giri General Store Shop No. 4, Krishna Menon Lane, New Delhi. R/o 109 A Gautam Nagar, New Delhi A-2) Mukesh Kumar M/s Shyam Lal Raj Kumar, 4053/55, Naya Bazar, Delhi - 6 ... Accused persons Judgment: 1. The present is a complaint filed under section 16 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (herein after referred to as Pfa Act), alleging that the accused have violated the provisions of t",
    "facts": "Judgment: 1. The present is a complaint filed under section 16 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (herein after referred to as Pfa Act), alleging that the accused have violated the provisions of t he PFA Act and Rules. 2. As per the complaint, on 31.07.2002, the Food Inspector (herein after referred as FI) Sh Bal Mukand purchased a sample of 'Moong Dal (Dhuli)\" a food article for analysis from accused Bal Kishan at M/s Purna Giri General Store, Shop No. 4, Krishna Menon Lane, New Delhi -11, where the said food article was found stored for sale and where accused No.1 Bal Kishan was found conducting the business of the said food article at the time of sampling. The sample consisted 600 grams of Da vs Bal Kishan & Ors. on 26 July, 2018 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/103866749/ 1 Moong Dal(Dhuli) taken from an open gunny bag bearing no label declaration. The sample was taken under the supervision / direction of Sh. S.M. Haider, SDM/LHA. The sample was taken after proper homogenization with the help of clean and dry jhaba by rotating in all possible directions. The Food Inspector divided the sample then and there into three equal parts by putting into three clean and dry bottles. Each bottle was separately packed, fastened and sealed according to PFA Act and Rules. The vendor's signatures were obtained on the LHA slip and the wrapper of the sample bottles. Notice was given to accused No. 1 Bal Kishan and the price of sample was also given to him vide vendor receipt dated 31.07.2002. Panchnama too was prepared at the spot. All the documents prepared by F.I. were signed by accused No.1 Bal Kishan and the other witness Sh Jeet Ram, FI, Before starting sample proceeding efforts were made to join public witnesses but none came forward, as such FI Sh Jeet Ram was joined as witness. 3. One counter-part of the sample bearing LHA code No. SMH/LHA/004772 was also sent to Public Analyst, Delhi in intact DA vs Bal Kishan & Ors. condition and two counter-parts were deposited with the LHA in intact condition. Public Analyst analysed the sample and found the same to be adulterated. The report of the Public Analyst is as follows: \"The sample is adulterated because it is coloured with synthetic colouring matter viz tartarzine.\" 4. It is the case of the complainant that accused No.1 Bal Kishan was the vendor-cum-proprietor of M/s Purna Giri General Store, Shop No. 4, Krishna Menon Lane, New Delhi -11 at the time of sampling. The vendor disclosed that the sample commodity was purchased from accused No. 2 Mukesh Kumar M/s Shyam Lal Raj Kumar, 4053/55, Naya Bazar, Delhi - 6 as such both the vendor and proprietor of supplier firm are being prosecuted. 5. Thereafter on completion of investigation, sanction under section 20 of the PFA Act was obtained from the Director PFA. The complaint was then filed in the court on 02.04.2003 alleging violation of section 2(ia)(j)&(m) of PFA Act, 1954 and Rule 23 read with Rule 28 & 29 of PFA Rules, 1955 as punishable section 7/16(1)(a) of PFA Act against the accused. 6. As the complaint was filed in writing by a public servant, recording of pre-summoning evidence was dispensed with and the accused were summoned vide order dated 02.04.2003. The accused thereafter exercised their option of analysing the counter part through CFL under section 13 (2) of the PFA Act. Based on the report of the CFL as well as evidence led in pre charge evidence, charge was framed against the accused for commission of the DA vs Bal Kishan & Ors. offence punishable under section 7/16(1) (A) PFA Act being violation of Section 2(ia)(a)(b)(j)&(m) and Rule 23 read with Rule 28 & 29 of PFA Rules, 1955, to which accused pleaded not guilty and claimed trial. Da vs Bal Kishan & Ors. on 26 July, 2018 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/103866749/ 2 7. At the trial, the prosecution examined three witness in support of its case i.e. PW-1 SDM/LHA Sh S.M. Haider, PW-2 FI Bal Mukand and PW-3 FI Jeet Ram. PW-2 and PW-3 were part of the team that had visited the spot for sample proceedings under the supervision of SDM/LHA Sh.S.M. Haider. These witness narrated the steps undertaken by them during the sample proceedings, including disclosing their identity, expressing intention to purchase sample for analysis, lifting the sample commodity of 600 grams of \"Moong Dal(Dhuli)\" from the vendor for analysis which was lying in an open gunny bag, bearing no label declaration, dividing the sample into three equal parts by putting them into three clean and dry glass bottles, separately sealing, packing and marking the same and obtaining signatures of vendor and witnesses. They also proved the necessary documents including the vendor's receipt Ex.PW-1/A, Notice Ex. PW-1/B, Panchnama Ex.PW-1/C. The vendor gave his statement Ex.PW1/D and also furnished photocopy of bill Mark A. Thereafter, on 01.08.2002 one counterpart of the sample along with one copy of memo in form VII and another copy of memo in form VII in sealed deposited with PA vide receipt Ex.PW1/F and the remaining two counter parts of the sample in intact condition along with two copies of memo of Form VII in a sealed packet were deposited with LHA vide LHA Receipt Ex.PW1/E under intimation that one counterpart of the same has already been deposited with PA. Thereafter, PA Report DA vs Bal Kishan & Ors. Ex.PW1/G was received, according to which the sample was adulterated because it is coloured with synthetic colouring matter vix tartrazine. Thereafter PW-2 FI Bal Mukand further investigated the matter and sent letter Ex.PW2/A to STO, Ward No. 99 and received its reply stating that accused Bal Kishan was the proprietor of M/s Purna Giri Genral Store. PW-2 also received letter Ex.PW2/B from the accused Bal Kishan alongwith documents Mark B & C. PW-2 FI Bal Mukand also received a reply vide Ex.PW2/D from STO Ward No. 29 stating that accused Mukesh Kumar was the Proprietor of M/s Shyam Lal Raj Kumar. Notice under section 14 A Ex.PW2/E addressed to Shyam Lal Raj Kumar was also prepared at the spot. After completion of investigation, the entire case file was put before then Director PFA for obtaining his Consent. The then Director PFA granted his Consent for prosecution vide sanction order Ex.PW1/H. Thereafter, the present complaint Ex.PW1/J was filed before the Court. Then intimation letter Ex.PW1/K alongwith copy of PA Reports was sent to the accused vide postal receipt Ex.PW1/L which was received back undelivered. These witnesses were duly cross-examined by Ld. Defence Counsel. 8. Statement of the accused under section 313 CrPC were recorded on 02.08.2013 wherein the accused persons denied the allegations and pleaded innocence. Thereafter matter was listed for defence evidence. 9. In defence, the accused No. 2 Mukesh Kumar examined himself as DW-1 and placed on record challan Ex.DW1/A and copy of bill Ex.DW1/B. The accused No. 1 Bal Kishan also examined himself DA vs Bal Kishan & Ors. as DW-2. Thereafter defence evidence was closed vide order dated 29.07.2016 and the matter was fixed for final arguments. on 26 July, 2018 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/103866749/ 3 10.",
    "arguments": "Under section 2(ia)(a) of PFA Act, the prosecution has to establish that the purchaser had demanded a food article of a specific nature, substance or quality and the article sold was, to his prejudice, either not of the nature, substance or quality demanded, or was not of the nature, substance or quality which it purported or represented to be.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Under section 2(ia)(a) of PFA Act, the prosecution has to establish that the purchaser had demanded a food article of a specific nature, substance or quality and the article sold was, to his prejudice, either not of the nature, substance or quality demanded, or was not of the nature, substance or quality which it purported or represented to be."
